package lumi:ext@0.3.0;

/// What an extension may be handed, cut into interfaces by capability.
///
/// The cut matters more than the contents: an interface here is the unit a
/// manifest capability grants, and each interface's doc says what it
/// costs. An extension reaches exactly what one of Lumi's own flows could
/// reach through the same capability — never more, and never by a
/// different road.
///
/// v0.3, the second break: `on-event` joined the exports. A world's exports
/// are what the host requires, so a component built against 0.2 — which
/// has no such export — does not fit this one and is refused at install
/// with a sentence saying to rebuild it, exactly as 0.1 was when 0.2 added
/// `on-lifecycle`. `on-event` is built so that it is the last export this
/// shape of change needs: a new kind of event is a new name and a JSON
/// payload with a `"v"` field, never another export. From here 0.3 is the
/// wire commitment, not a moving target.
///
/// Lumi checks the grant at every call rather than refusing to load a
/// component that imports an interface its manifest did not ask for: a
/// component imports what its code calls, and an interface imported is not
/// yet an interface used, so a load-time refusal would say nothing a call
/// would not say better. The enforcement is in Lumi, at the call, not in
/// the review sheet. A breaking
/// change to an interface bumps this version and adds a new world Lumi
/// keeps loadable alongside it — Zed's model, per `extensions/STORE.md`.

/// The selected text in the frontmost application.
///
/// Costs `accessibility` — and reading it through the ⌘C fallback costs
/// `clipboard` too, so the strategy follows the grant: both granted tries
/// Accessibility first and falls back to a ⌘C, the way Lumi's own flows
/// read a selection; `accessibility` alone is the Accessibility-only read
/// that never touches the pasteboard.
interface selection {
    /// How the text was arrived at: "accessibility", "copy", or
    /// "nothing".
    record found {
        text: string,
        how: string,
    }

    read: func() -> result<found, string>;
}

/// The general pasteboard. Costs `clipboard`, both directions — and
/// `paste` costs more, because it presses a key in somebody else's app.
interface clipboard {
    /// The board's plain text, or none when it holds no text — an image, a
    /// file, or nothing at all are one answer here: no text.
    read-text: func() -> result<option<string>, string>;

    write-text: func(text: string) -> result<_, string>;

    /// The bytes of one representation.
    variant data {
        text(string),
        bytes(list<u8>),
        /// A blob in this extension's own `storage`, by id — so an image
        /// the extension keeps goes back on the board without crossing
        /// into the component and out again.
        blob(string),
    }

    /// One representation of one pasteboard item: its type, as a uniform
    /// type identifier (`public.utf8-plain-text`, `public.png`, …), and
    /// its bytes. An item is a list of these — the same thing in several
    /// types, the richest first.
    record rep {
        uti: string,
        data: data,
    }

    /// Replace the board with these items. At most 160 MB in all. Lumi
    /// marks the board as written by this extension, so the extension can
    /// tell its own writes apart when it hears a copy. Added in 0.3.
    write-all: func(items: list<list<rep>>) -> result<_, string>;

    /// `write-all`, then ⌘V into the app in front: what a clipboard
    /// history does when a row is picked. Closes this extension's panels
    /// first, so the keystroke lands where the caret was. The board keeps
    /// what was pasted; it is not put back.
    ///
    /// Costs `clipboard`, `input` and `accessibility`. Answers only
    /// something the person did — a command or one of the extension's
    /// windows — and is refused from `on-event` and `on-lifecycle`, where
    /// nobody asked for a keystroke. Added in 0.3.
    paste: func(items: list<list<rep>>) -> result<_, string>;

    /// What a `drag` does once the items are dropped.
    record drag-options {
        /// Take this extension's panels down after a drop that took the
        /// items. A drag given up — dropped back, or on nothing that wants
        /// it — never closes anything.
        close-on-drop: bool,
    }

    /// Start a drag of these items out of the extension's window that the
    /// person is pressing in, into whatever application they drop it on —
    /// Finder, a browser's upload field, a chat. An item with a file URL
    /// carries the file; an image with no file becomes a PNG file where a
    /// file is wanted (Finder, the desktop) and stays an image everywhere
    /// else; anything else goes as its types, like `write-all`. The board
    /// the person copies with is not touched. At most 160 MB in all.
    ///
    /// Costs `clipboard`. Answers only one of the extension's windows the
    /// person is using, and only while the mouse button is still down in
    /// it: refused otherwise, so an extension cannot start a drag nobody is
    /// making. Call it from the page when a press on an item starts to move.
    /// Added in Lumi 1.26.
    drag: func(items: list<list<rep>>, options: drag-options) -> result<_, string>;
}

/// Lumi's own alert overlay, styled from Settings. Costs no capability,
/// and one call of the run's budget: it draws over the screen the way any
/// of Lumi's own overlays do, reaches no other process, and is how an
/// extension reports to the person at the keyboard. Free of permission is
/// not free of budget, which is what stops a loop of alerts flooding the
/// screen.
interface alert {
    show: func(text: string) -> result<_, string>;
}

/// Hand a URL to whatever application claimed its scheme.
///
/// Web and mail addresses only — the URL was assembled by a component out
/// of values it read off the desk, so it gets the same narrow allowlist
/// Lumi keeps for a URL whose scheme came out of another application's
/// window, rather than the any-scheme door of a URL the person typed
/// themselves. Costs `applications`; a web address costs `network` too, the
/// same split Lumi's own Open URL action makes — except for a URL opened for
/// a press (a command, or the extension's own window), which the person is
/// opening themselves and costs nothing but the call.
interface open {
    url: func(target: string) -> result<_, string>;

    /// Select one file in Finder, bringing Finder forward. `target` is a
    /// `file:` URL — Finder's file-reference form included, which is what a
    /// copied file carries — or an absolute path. Only for something the
    /// person did: a command they pressed or a press in one of the
    /// extension's windows. Costs no capability. Added in 0.3.
    reveal: func(target: string) -> result<_, string>;
}

/// The extension's own settings, as saved in the Extensions pane.
///
/// Costs no capability, and one call of the run's budget: the values were
/// typed into a form the extension's own manifest declared, so reading them
/// back reaches nobody's desk. A read past the budget has no error to give
/// in this shape and answers the empty object, so read early in a run. The
/// answer is a JSON object as text, one key per declared field, with the
/// manifest's defaults already filled in for anything the person never
/// touched — an extension never has to know whether a value was chosen
/// or defaulted, which is the same contract an untouched form field keeps
/// in the sheet. A field the manifest declares `scope = "profile"` holds
/// the live profile's value, so the same read serves every profile.
interface settings {
    read: func() -> string;
}

/// The person's profiles, and which one is live.
///
/// A profile is a whole set of automations — shortcuts, menus, snippets,
/// flows — that the person switches between, one live at a time: work and
/// home, or one per client. An extension is installed once for the whole
/// Mac, so this is how it tells the sets apart: to keep a value per profile
/// in its own storage, to word something by the set the person is in, or to
/// offer a choice between them in one of its windows.
///
/// Costs no capability, and one call of the run's budget: the names are
/// labels the person typed for their own sets, and reading them reaches no
/// other application. One read answers both
/// halves, so a switch landing between two calls can never pair one
/// profile's list with another moment's live id. Read it when you need it —
/// the person may switch at any time, and nothing tells a component that
/// they did.
interface profiles {
    record profile {
        /// Stable for the life of the profile: a rename keeps it, and it is
        /// never reused while the profile exists. The key to store by.
        id: string,
        /// What the person calls it. Renamed at will — show it, never
        /// store by it.
        name: string,
    }

    record book {
        /// The `id` of the live profile — always one of `profiles`.
        active: string,
        /// Every profile on this Mac, in the order Lumi lists them. Never
        /// empty: a Mac that never made a second profile has one.
        profiles: list<profile>,
    }

    read: func() -> result<book, string>;
}

/// Lumi itself: which build is running, and whether this Mac has Pro.
///
/// `about` and `license` cost nothing at all, not even a call of the
/// budget: none of it is anybody's content, and an over-budget answer would
/// have to invent a version or an edition. `hyper-key-enabled` costs no
/// capability and one call, since it reads the live document.
///
/// The version is what an extension checks before relying on something a
/// newer Lumi added; the edition is what lets it word a feature by what the
/// person has. The licence key, the seat, and who bought it are not here
/// and never will be — the edition is the whole of what an extension
/// learns about the licence.
interface app {
    record info {
        /// Lumi's version, "major.minor.patch" — compare it rather than
        /// match it.
        version: string,
        /// Lumi's homepage, as a full URL.
        homepage: string,
        /// Who makes Lumi.
        author: string,
    }

    enum edition {
        /// No licence on this Mac.
        free,
        /// Pro is on.
        pro,
        /// A licence is on this Mac and is not granting Pro right now —
        /// ended, refused, or not checked for too long. Which one is the
        /// person's business in Lumi's own License pane, not an
        /// extension's.
        inactive,
    }

    about: func() -> info;
    /// This Mac's edition — the only thing about the licence an extension
    /// is ever told.
    license: func() -> edition;

    /// Whether the Hyper key is switched on in the live profile. While it
    /// is, Caps Lock is Lumi's: remapped below every application and never
    /// delivered as itself, so an extension that listens for keys — a
    /// keyboard tester — has to know not to wait for it. One switch and
    /// nothing else: which modifiers it adds, what a tap does and every
    /// shortcut it holds stay behind `config`. Costs no capability.
    ///
    /// Added to 0.2 without a new world version: an existing component
    /// imports nothing new and is unaffected. A component that calls this
    /// needs a Lumi that has it, which its manifest's `min-lumi-version`
    /// says.
    hyper-key-enabled: func() -> result<bool, string>;
}

/// The person's automations and Lumi's settings, read-only, as the live
/// profile holds them. Costs `config`.
///
/// Each answer is a JSON object as text, in the same camelCase fields and
/// values Lumi saves the document with, so what you learn reading one
/// answer holds for the next Lumi too. Read what you need when you need
/// it: the person may edit or switch profile at any time, and nothing tells
/// a component that they did. Rows written by a newer Lumi that this one
/// cannot read are left out rather than guessed at.
///
/// There is no write. An extension that wants to change what happens on a
/// keystroke offers a command or a node the person binds; this is how it
/// fits around what they already have. Refused with a sentence while
/// Lumi's own settings file cannot be read, rather than answering with
/// empty defaults that would read as "this person has nothing".
interface config {
    /// Lumi's general settings: `appearance`, `startAtLogin`,
    /// `showProfileInMenuBar`, `appShortcuts`, `alert`, `leader`,
    /// `arrange`.
    settings: func() -> result<string, string>;
    /// `{"enabled": bool, "bindings": [...]}` — the Shortcuts pane's switch
    /// and every row: ordinary combinations, double-taps, Fn combinations,
    /// leader menus and their steps.
    shortcuts: func() -> result<string, string>;
    /// Snippet expansion: its switch, its options, and every snippet.
    snippets: func() -> result<string, string>;
    /// The Caps Lock Hyper key.
    hyper-key: func() -> result<string, string>;
    /// Double-tap: its switch and timing, plus the rows it arms under
    /// `bindings` — the same rows `shortcuts` lists, picked out.
    double-tap: func() -> result<string, string>;
    /// Shortcuts held on the Fn key: the switch, plus the rows it arms
    /// under `bindings`, picked out the same way.
    fn-key: func() -> result<string, string>;

    /// Your own `[[shortcut]]`s, as Lumi holds them: a list, one per
    /// declaration in manifest order, each `{"command", "label", "key",
    /// "declared", "state", "reason", "holder"}`. `key` is the accelerator
    /// armed now or null; `declared` the manifest's; `state` is
    /// "registered", "taken" (something held `declared` at install —
    /// `holder` says what, `reason` says it in a sentence), "invalid" (a
    /// key Lumi cannot register) or "cleared". Costs nothing: they are
    /// yours, and you learn nothing of anybody else's. Lumi tries each
    /// key once, at install, and never chooses between your key and a
    /// row of the person's — read this, and decide.
    extension-shortcuts: func() -> result<string, string>;

    /// Arm, change or clear one of your own `[[shortcut]]`s: the answer
    /// to what `extension-shortcuts` reported, or a change the person
    /// asked for in a window of yours. `key` is an accelerator in the
    /// recorder's spelling (`Shift+Super+KeyC`), or none to clear. When
    /// something holds the key the call is refused with a sentence
    /// naming it, unless `replace` — which takes the key off the
    /// person's row (in whichever profile) or Lumi's own; another
    /// extension's key is never taken. Answers the entry as it now
    /// stands, in `extension-shortcuts`' shape. Costs nothing, for the
    /// same reason: only a command of yours can be named.
    set-extension-shortcut: func(command: string, key: option<string>, replace: bool) -> result<string, string>;
}

/// The extension's own windows, as declared under `[[window]]`.
///
/// Costs nothing beyond budget: a window here is the extension's own UI,
/// drawn from the `ui/` files that shipped inside the reviewed package,
/// in a webview with no access to Lumi's internals, whose only reach is
/// the bridge back into this same component. The gate is the declaration —
/// a name the manifest does not declare is refused, so the review sheet's
/// list of windows is also the list of windows that can ever open.
interface ui {
    /// Open (or focus) one declared window by its manifest `name`. One
    /// already up is focused, never closed, whoever asks — your command's
    /// own press included: a pinned panel the person left takes the
    /// keyboard back where it stands, any other comes to the pointer. A key
    /// that also puts a window away is yours to build, from `window-state`
    /// and `close-window`. Until Lumi 1.31 a press on a panel that held the
    /// keyboard closed it instead.
    open-window: func(name: string) -> result<_, string>;

    /// Close one declared window by its manifest `name`. One that is not
    /// open is not an error. Added in 0.3.
    close-window: func(name: string) -> result<_, string>;

    /// Where one of your windows stands.
    enum presence {
        /// Not on screen — a panel put away included.
        hidden,
        /// On screen while the keyboard is elsewhere: a pinned panel once
        /// the person works in another application. A pointer resting over
        /// it is not the keyboard — the panel stays `up`.
        up,
        /// On screen with the keyboard.
        focused,
    }

    /// Where one declared window stands, by its manifest `name` — for a
    /// command that decides for itself: a toggle that puts a window away
    /// when it is `focused`, or a show that only nudges one already in
    /// front. Allowed wherever `post` is, `on-event` included. Added in
    /// Lumi 1.31.
    window-state: func(name: string) -> result<presence, string>;

    /// Bring up Lumi's Settings on this extension's own Settings tab — a
    /// panel's "Settings…". Only for something the person did. Added in 0.3.
    open-settings: func() -> result<_, string>;

    /// Choose the glass one of your panels opens on from now on:
    /// "popover", "hud" or "sidebar" — for a setting that lets the person
    /// pick. Only a panel whose manifest declares a `material`. Takes effect
    /// the next time the panel is shown. Added in 0.3.
    set-material: func(name: string, material: string) -> result<_, string>;

    /// Choose light or dark for one of your panels: "light", "dark", or
    /// "system" to follow macOS again — for a theme setting. The panel's
    /// glass and its page's `prefers-color-scheme` both follow. Takes effect
    /// at once if the panel is up, and every time it is shown. Added in 0.3.
    set-theme: func(name: string, theme: string) -> result<_, string>;

    /// Pin one of your panels, or let it go: a pinned panel stays up while
    /// the person works in another application, instead of closing the
    /// moment it loses the keyboard. A paste from it hands the keyboard
    /// back and leaves it up; it sits above ordinary windows but under
    /// menus. Escape and `close-window` still put it away, and the pin goes
    /// with it — every show starts unpinned. Your shortcut does not, from
    /// Lumi 1.31: it focuses the panel (`open-window`). Only a panel that is
    /// up, and only for something the person did in one of your windows.
    /// Added in Lumi 1.29.
    set-pinned: func(name: string, pinned: bool) -> result<_, string>;

    /// Make one of your windows' unified title bar `height` points tall —
    /// taller or shorter than the manifest's `titlebar-height` — and macOS's
    /// traffic lights move to stay centred in it. Answers the height it
    /// became, clamped to 32–96 as the manifest's is; draw your toolbar at
    /// that. Only a window declared `titlebar = "unified"` that is open; the
    /// next open starts from the manifest's height again. Added in Lumi 1.30.
    set-titlebar-height: func(name: string, height: f64) -> result<f64, string>;

    /// Send `message`, JSON text, to one of this extension's own windows
    /// or embedded pages: a declared window by its manifest `name`, or a
    /// page by `:about`, `:settings` or `:page:<name>`. It arrives as a
    /// `lumi:message` event on the page's `window`, its `detail` the parsed
    /// JSON. `false` when that window or page is not on screen, so nothing
    /// was sent — a post is not queued. The one way to reach a page while
    /// Lumi is not in front, from `on-event` included: data flows to the
    /// page and nothing flows back. Refused over 64 KiB or when `message`
    /// is not JSON. Added in 0.3.
    post: func(window: string, message: string) -> result<bool, string>;
}

/// One HTTP request. Costs `network`.
interface net {
    record request {
        /// Upper-cased by the host; an empty string is GET.
        method: string,
        url: string,
        headers: list<tuple<string, string>>,
        body: option<string>,
    }

    record response {
        status: u16,
        /// `status` in 200..300, the same thing `fetch` means by it.
        ok: bool,
        /// The final URL, which differs from the asked-for one after a
        /// redirect.
        url: string,
        headers: list<tuple<string, string>>,
        body: string,
    }

    fetch: func(request: request) -> result<response, string>;
}

/// This extension's own storage: a small key–value store and a store of
/// blobs, kept by Lumi on this Mac and encrypted with a key Lumi holds in
/// the Keychain — the component never sees it.
///
/// Costs no capability: it reaches nothing outside this extension, and no
/// other extension can read it. It survives updates, is removed when the
/// extension is uninstalled, and is not part of a Lumi backup.
///
/// Key names are **not** encrypted — they are file names. Put what matters
/// in the value, and use random ids where a name would say something.
/// Names are 1–128 lowercase letters, digits, `.`, `-` or `_`, not starting
/// with `.`. A value is at most 1 MB; the whole store, 512 MB.
///
/// Added in 0.3.
interface storage {
    record entry {
        value: string,
        /// Bumped by every write. Hand it back to `put` to write only if
        /// nothing else wrote in between.
        rev: u64,
    }

    record space {
        /// Bytes on disk, encryption included.
        bytes: u64,
        limit: u64,
    }

    get: func(key: string) -> result<option<entry>, string>;
    /// Write `value` if `key` is still at `if-rev` — `none` meaning it must
    /// not exist yet. Answers the new revision. Fails with exactly
    /// `"conflict"` when something else wrote first: read again, reapply,
    /// write again.
    put: func(key: string, value: string, if-rev: option<u64>) -> result<u64, string>;
    /// Removing a key that is not there is not an error.
    delete: func(key: string) -> result<_, string>;
    /// Every key starting with `prefix`, sorted.
    keys: func(prefix: string) -> result<list<string>, string>;
    /// Store bytes (at most 4 MB from a component) and answer their id.
    blob-write: func(bytes: list<u8>) -> result<string, string>;
    /// A blob by id. Lumi may write larger blobs than a component can read
    /// back; those are for pages to show, not for the component to load.
    blob-read: func(id: string) -> result<list<u8>, string>;
    blob-delete: func(id: string) -> result<_, string>;
    /// Hand one blob to the person as a file: this extension's panels go
    /// off screen, and Lumi's own Save panel comes up with `name` filled
    /// in and the extension's name in its message. Answers once the Save
    /// panel is asked for, not once it is answered — where the file went,
    /// or that the person cancelled, is theirs and is not told back; a
    /// write that fails is reported to them in an alert.
    ///
    /// `name` is a file name, not a path: 1–255 bytes, no `/`, no control
    /// characters, not starting with `.`. Any size of blob, Lumi's own
    /// larger ones included — the bytes never cross into the component.
    /// Only for something the person did: a command they pressed or a
    /// press in one of the extension's windows. Added in Lumi 1.26.
    blob-save: func(id: string, name: string) -> result<_, string>;
    /// `blob-save`, with every `{date}` and `{time}` in `name` filled in
    /// from this Mac's clock and time zone — `2026-10-02` and `14.03.11`,
    /// the way macOS names a screenshot — before the name is checked. For a
    /// component, which knows the time only in UTC. The name as filled in is
    /// never told back: handed back beside the clock the component already
    /// has, it would say the person's time zone. Added in Lumi 1.34.
    blob-save-dated: func(id: string, name: string) -> result<_, string>;
    /// Every blob and its size in bytes, sorted by id.
    blobs: func() -> result<list<tuple<string, u64>>, string>;
    usage: func() -> result<space, string>;
}

/// The pixels on screen. Costs `screen` — and macOS's Screen Recording
/// permission, which is Lumi's: granted once, in System Settings, for every
/// extension that declares `screen`. The capability is what Lumi lets the
/// extension do; the permission is what macOS lets Lumi do. A capture
/// without the permission is refused with a sentence saying where to turn
/// it on; nothing here asks macOS for it, because a dialog is only shown
/// while Lumi's own settings window is in front.
///
/// Coordinates are points, origin at the top-left of the main display, y
/// down — the space every display and window frame on macOS is reported
/// in. Lumi's own windows, this extension's included, are never in the
/// picture: the one asking is usually the one with a window up.
///
/// Needs macOS 14; below it every capture is refused by name. Added to 0.3
/// in Lumi 1.30 without a new world version: an existing component imports
/// nothing new and is unaffected, and one that calls this needs a Lumi that
/// has it, which its manifest's `min-lumi-version` says.
interface screen {
    record rect {
        x: f64,
        y: f64,
        width: f64,
        height: f64,
    }

    /// Which window to capture, and how.
    record window {
        /// The id `select-window` answered — or `none` for the frontmost
        /// ordinary window that is not Lumi's.
        id: option<u32>,
        /// The shadow macOS draws round the window too, in the picture's
        /// alpha, as far as it reaches — tens of points on each side, most
        /// below. A window whose shadow cannot be placed is taken without
        /// it; `frame` tells: it is the window's own frame then.
        shadow: bool,
    }

    variant target {
        /// The whole display the pointer is on.
        display,
        /// One window, cut to what it draws: an application's window is
        /// all of it, its rounded corners transparent; the Dock is its bar,
        /// not the display-sized window macOS draws it on. The menu bar,
        /// which macOS keeps no picture of, is what is on screen where it
        /// is. `frame` says where the picture is. Refused if the window
        /// has closed since.
        window(window),
        /// This area, clipped to the one display it covers most of.
        area(rect),
    }

    /// A window the person picked.
    record picked-window {
        /// The window server's number for it: hand it to `capture` as a
        /// `window`'s `id`.
        id: u32,
        /// Where it is on screen, in points.
        frame: rect,
    }

    /// The file a picture is written as.
    enum format {
        /// Lossless, transparency kept: a window's rounded corners and its
        /// shadow stay transparent. `public.png` on the clipboard.
        png,
        /// Smaller, at `quality`. JPEG holds no transparency, so a
        /// picture is laid on white first — a window's corners and shadow
        /// are drawn over white, as macOS's own JPEG screenshots are.
        /// `public.jpeg` on the clipboard.
        jpeg,
    }

    /// How many pixels a point of the screen becomes.
    enum resolution {
        /// The display's own: two pixels a point on a Retina display.
        native,
        /// One pixel a point, the size the picture had on screen.
        points,
    }

    /// How `capture-as` hands a picture over.
    record encoding {
        format: format,
        /// For `jpeg`, from 1 (smallest) to 100 (best); outside that range
        /// it is brought inside. Not read for `png`.
        quality: u8,
        resolution: resolution,
    }

    record shot {
        /// The picture, in this extension's storage — a PNG from `capture`,
        /// the encoding asked for from `capture-as`: a page shows it at
        /// `/__lumi__/blob/<id>`, `clipboard` takes it as `blob`, and
        /// `storage.blob-save` hands it to the person. It counts against
        /// the store's space until it is deleted.
        blob: string,
        /// In pixels: at the display's own resolution, or one a point for
        /// `resolution.points`.
        width: u32,
        height: u32,
        /// Pixels per point.
        scale: f64,
        /// Where on screen the picture came from, in points.
        frame: rect,
    }

    /// Whether Lumi holds Screen Recording. Takes no picture, and is
    /// right for a permission given since Lumi started too — for a status
    /// line, say. Without the permission `capture`, `select-area` and
    /// `select-window` are refused, and Lumi itself asks for it: your
    /// extension never does, and need not ask this first. The same answer
    /// as `permissions.check(screen-recording)`, which comes with the rest
    /// of the toolset for asking first.
    permitted: func() -> result<bool, string>;
    /// Take one picture. Only for something the person did — a command
    /// they pressed or a press in one of the extension's windows — never
    /// from `on-event` or a lifecycle hook.
    capture: func(target: target) -> result<shot, string>;
    /// `capture`, written as `encoding` says: the picture is taken at that
    /// resolution and encoded once, by Lumi, so an extension with no page
    /// on screen can still hand over a JPEG or a picture at screen size.
    /// The same rules as `capture`. Added in Lumi 1.34.
    capture-as: func(target: target, encoding: encoding) -> result<shot, string>;
    /// Let the person drag out an area: every display dims, the pointer
    /// becomes a crosshair, and the area they drag is the answer — `none`
    /// for Escape, a right click, or two minutes with no answer. Takes no
    /// picture; hand the area to `capture` for one. Refused at once without
    /// Screen Recording, as `capture` would be — nobody drags out an area
    /// that cannot be captured. The time the person takes is not counted
    /// against the run. Only for something the person did, as `capture`.
    select-area: func() -> result<option<rect>, string>;
    /// Let the person pick a window: every display dims, the window under
    /// the pointer lights up, and the one they click is the answer — `none`
    /// for Escape, a right click, or two minutes with no answer.
    /// Applications' windows, the menu bar, its items and the Dock are
    /// offered; Lumi's own windows never are. Takes no picture; hand the id
    /// to `capture` as a `window`'s for one. Refused at once without Screen
    /// Recording, as `select-area` is. The person's time is not counted
    /// against the run, and only for something the person did, as
    /// `select-area`.
    select-window: func() -> result<option<picked-window>, string>;
}

/// The person's snippets, asked about a whole text: is it a trigger, and
/// what does it expand to. Costs `snippets`. For a component holding text
/// from somewhere other than the keyboard — a copy in a history, a line in
/// a document — that wants to say what Lumi would make of it.
///
/// A snippet counts when it is switched on and has a trigger. Where and when
/// it would fire while typing — its applications, the profile's own
/// Snippets switch, on a space or at once — is not asked: nothing is being
/// typed, and what the component does with the answer is its own.
///
/// Added to 0.3 in Lumi 1.31 without a new world version: an existing
/// component imports nothing new and is unaffected, and one that calls this
/// needs a Lumi that has it, which its manifest's `min-lumi-version` says.
interface snippets {
    /// Whose snippets to look in. Profile ids are `profiles.read`'s.
    variant within {
        /// The live profile's.
        active,
        /// Every profile's, in the order Lumi lists them.
        all,
        /// These profiles', in the order Lumi lists them. An id no profile
        /// has is skipped.
        only(list<string>),
    }

    /// One snippet whose trigger is the whole of the text asked about.
    record hit {
        /// The profile it is in.
        profile: string,
        /// Its id, stable while it exists — what `expand` takes.
        snippet: string,
        /// As the person wrote it: literal text, or a regular expression.
        trigger: string,
        /// Changes whenever anything that decides its expansion does — the
        /// trigger, the replacement, or how either is read. Keep an
        /// expansion under it, and keep it no longer than this stays the
        /// same.
        revision: string,
        /// Whether the expansion is the same every time: plain text with no
        /// `{{$…}}` variable and no script. Only then is one worth keeping.
        fixed: bool,
    }

    record expansion {
        /// What the trigger expands to — plain text, always.
        text: string,
        /// It holds something read from the clipboard, through
        /// `{{$clipboardText}}` or a script that read it. Answered only to
        /// a component that also holds `clipboard`; anyone else is refused.
        sealed: bool,
        /// `hit.revision` and `hit.fixed` as they stand now, for a snippet
        /// changed between the two calls.
        revision: string,
        fixed: bool,
    }

    /// Every snippet in those profiles whose trigger is the whole of `text`,
    /// as typed: with the snippet's own case sensitivity, a regular
    /// expression matching from its first character to its last. Profile by
    /// profile, then in the order each lists them. A text longer than any
    /// trigger can be typed — 64 characters — matches nothing, and a profile
    /// whose snippets cannot be read is skipped.
    find: func(text: string, within: within) -> result<list<hit>, string>;
    /// Expand one snippet as typing `text` would: its variables rendered,
    /// its script run against what a pattern caught. Refused when `text` is
    /// no longer its trigger, or it is gone or switched off.
    expand: func(profile: string, snippet: string, text: string) -> result<expansion, string>;
}

/// macOS permissions Lumi holds on behalf of every extension. The grant is
/// always Lumi's — macOS credits the app, never an extension inside it — so
/// your extension asks Lumi, and Lumi asks macOS.
///
/// Two ways to use it. The one-liner: `ask`, and Lumi shows its own sheet in
/// Settings, with your extension's name, walking the person through macOS's
/// questions. Or draw your own guidance in one of your windows and build it
/// from `check`, `request` and `open-system-settings`.
///
/// Each permission is reachable only by an extension declaring the
/// capability that needs it — screen-recording needs `screen` — so nobody is
/// asked for a permission the review sheet did not show. Every call costs
/// budget; `ask`, `request` and `open-system-settings` also answer only
/// something the person did — a command or a press in one of your windows —
/// never `on-event` or a lifecycle hook.
///
/// Added to 0.3 in Lumi 1.33 without a new world version: an existing
/// component imports nothing new and is unaffected, and one that calls this
/// needs a Lumi that has it, which its manifest's `min-lumi-version` says.
interface permissions {
    enum permission {
        /// Capturing the screen — `screen.capture`, `select-area`,
        /// `select-window`. Needs the `screen` capability.
        screen-recording,
    }

    enum state {
        granted,
        not-granted,
    }

    /// What `request` did.
    enum requested {
        /// macOS was asked. Its dialog shows once in Lumi's life; after that
        /// asking is silent, and the switch is in System Settings.
        asked,
        /// Nothing to ask: Lumi already has it.
        already-granted,
        /// Not asked: Lumi's flow editor has unsaved changes, and turning
        /// the permission on can offer to quit Lumi, which would stop to ask
        /// about them. Ask the person to save or close the flow, or call
        /// `ask`, whose sheet says so.
        flow-editor-open,
        /// Not asked: no window of Lumi's is up — a panel never makes one —
        /// and macOS only lists an app that asked from a window. Ask from one
        /// of your windows, or call `ask`.
        no-window,
    }

    /// Whether Lumi holds it now. Takes no picture and asks nothing.
    check: func(p: permission) -> result<state, string>;
    /// Lumi's own sheet in Settings, worded with your extension's name. Comes
    /// back as soon as the sheet is up; the person answers in their own time.
    /// Does nothing when Lumi already holds the permission.
    ask: func(p: permission) -> result<_, string>;
    /// macOS's own request, for a page of your own that has explained it
    /// first. Say before calling it that macOS's first button is Deny.
    request: func(p: permission) -> result<requested, string>;
    /// System Settings at the permission's list, where Lumi's switch is.
    open-system-settings: func(p: permission) -> result<_, string>;
}

/// Folders the person chose for this extension to write files into — a
/// screenshot saved straight to a folder, an export, a backup.
///
/// Each is declared in the manifest by name, `[[folder]] name = "saves"
/// label = "Save screenshots to"`, and the review sheet lists it: a name the
/// manifest does not declare is refused. The person picks the folder itself
/// in Lumi's own panel, from your `choose` or from the Permissions tab, and
/// Lumi keeps it — your component is told the folder's name ("Desktop") and
/// never its path. It follows the folder through a rename or a move.
///
/// Write only, and never over a file: a name already taken is written as
/// "Name 2". Free of capability — the declaration is the grant — and every
/// call costs budget. Added to 0.3 in Lumi 1.34 without a new world version.
interface folders {
    /// Why a write did not happen, in the three cases worth answering
    /// differently: offer `choose`, say the folder moved away, or show the
    /// sentence.
    variant problem {
        /// None is chosen yet, or the person forgot it.
        not-chosen,
        /// The folder chosen is no longer there.
        gone,
        refused(string),
    }

    record chosen {
        /// The folder's own name, such as "Desktop" — for "Save to Desktop".
        label: string,
    }

    /// The folder chosen for `name`, or `none`.
    status: func(name: string) -> result<option<chosen>, string>;
    /// Ask the person to choose a folder for `name`, in Lumi's Open panel
    /// — `none` when they cancel, which leaves the earlier choice in place.
    /// Only for something the person did: a command, or a press in one of
    /// your windows. Their time choosing is not counted against the run.
    choose: func(name: string) -> result<option<chosen>, string>;
    /// Forget the folder chosen for `name`. One never chosen is not an error.
    forget: func(name: string) -> result<_, string>;
    /// Write the blob `blob` from your storage into the folder chosen for
    /// `name`, as `file-name` — one name, no `/`, not starting with `.`, with
    /// every `{date}` and `{time}` filled in from the Mac's clock as
    /// `storage.blob-save-dated` fills them. A name already in the folder is
    /// written as "Name 2", "Name 3"; nothing is ever written over. Any size
    /// of blob: the bytes never pass through your component. Only for
    /// something the person did.
    write: func(name: string, file-name: string, blob: string) -> result<_, problem>;
    /// Show the folder chosen for `name` in Finder. Only for something the
    /// person did.
    reveal: func(name: string) -> result<_, string>;
}

/// Your extension's own rows in Lumi's menu bar menu, built while it runs.
///
/// Lumi draws them in a section of their own after Recent: one submenu
/// titled with your extension's name and icon, or — when the whole tree is a
/// single plain item — that item on its own, with your icon beside it, so
/// its label has to say what it does. Nothing you set lands among Lumi's own
/// rows. A press comes back through `run-ui` with `window` set to `:menu`
/// and `request` set to `{"v":1,"id":"<entry id>"}`: something the person
/// did, so it may open a window, paste, or ask for a permission.
///
/// Lumi keeps the last tree you set, across restarts and updates, and draws
/// it without running your code — set it once from `on-lifecycle` and again
/// whenever something it shows changes. Switching the extension off hides
/// it; uninstalling removes it. Lumi never flips a check row by itself: a
/// press is told to you, and the row shows what you set next.
///
/// Needs the `menu` capability. Every call costs budget. Added to 0.3 in
/// Lumi 1.33 without a new world version, the way `permissions` was.
interface menu {
    enum kind {
        item,
        /// An item with a tick when `checked`.
        check,
        separator,
        /// Holds the entries whose `parent` is its `id`. One left empty is
        /// not drawn.
        submenu,
    }

    record entry {
        /// Yours: unique within the tree, at most 64 bytes, and what a press
        /// tells you back. A separator needs one too.
        id: string,
        /// `none` for the top of your submenu, or the `id` of a `submenu`
        /// entry that comes earlier in the list. Two levels of submenu below
        /// your own at most.
        parent: option<string>,
        kind: kind,
        /// What the row says; ignored on a separator. Cut to the menu's width.
        label: string,
        /// A disabled row is drawn dimmed and cannot be pressed — the shape
        /// of a status line.
        enabled: bool,
        /// Read on a `check` entry only.
        checked: bool,
    }

    /// Replace your whole tree. At most 64 entries. An empty list is `clear`.
    set: func(entries: list<entry>) -> result<_, string>;
    /// Take your rows out of the menu.
    clear: func() -> result<_, string>;
}

world extension {
    import selection;
    import clipboard;
    import alert;
    import open;
    import net;
    import settings;
    import profiles;
    import app;
    import config;
    import ui;
    import storage;
    import screen;
    import snippets;
    import permissions;
    import menu;
    import folders;

    /// Run one of the commands the manifest declares.
    ///
    /// `params` is a JSON object as text — v0 keeps the boundary dumb; the
    /// host renders `{{$…}}` in the values before they arrive. The answer
    /// is a sentence for whoever invoked it, or a refusal the host reports
    /// the way a failed action is reported.
    export run-command: func(name: string, params: string) -> result<string, string>;

    /// Run one of the flow nodes the manifest declares.
    ///
    /// `params` is the node's resolved params as a JSON object; `items` is
    /// a JSON array of the incoming items' json (binary slots do not cross
    /// this boundary in v0). The answer is a JSON array of the outgoing
    /// items' json — an empty array kills the branch, which is the data-flow
    /// rule every built-in node lives under. An error fails the node the
    /// way a built-in's `NodeError` does.
    export run-node: func(name: string, params: string, items: string) -> result<string, string>;

    /// Answer one request from one of the extension's own windows.
    ///
    /// `window` is the declared window the request came from — the host
    /// resolved it from the webview itself, so it cannot be spoofed by the
    /// page — or `:menu` for a press on one of your `menu` rows.
    /// `request` is whatever the window's JavaScript posted to
    /// `/__lumi__/call`, and the answer is handed back as the response
    /// body — both opaque strings, JSON by convention, because the dialect
    /// between an extension and its own UI is nobody's business but the
    /// extension's. Runs under the same budget, capability gate and
    /// deadline as a command: the window is a face on the sandbox, never a
    /// door around it.
    export run-ui: func(window: string, request: string) -> result<string, string>;

    /// A moment in the extension's own life, as Lumi saw it happen.
    variant lifecycle {
        /// Installed just now, for the first time on this Mac — after the
        /// package's own installer, if it has one, has already written the
        /// settings it asked for.
        installed,
        /// Replaced by this version. The payload is the version it
        /// replaced, so a "What's new" can say since when.
        updated(string),
        /// About to be removed: the last run this extension gets. Its
        /// files go the moment this returns.
        uninstalling,
    }

    /// Hear about one lifecycle moment. Called only while the extension is
    /// switched on — off means none of its code runs, this included.
    ///
    /// Under the same capability gate as a command, with three differences
    /// that follow from nobody having pressed anything:
    ///
    /// - **The answer is never shown.** An error is written to Lumi's log
    ///   and goes no further; there is nobody at the keyboard who asked.
    /// - **It cannot veto.** An `uninstalling` that fails, traps or runs
    ///   out of time is logged, and the uninstall goes ahead.
    /// - **`uninstalling` is short and has no windows.** The person is
    ///   waiting on the Uninstall button, so the deadline is a few seconds
    ///   rather than a command's; and the `ui/` files are about to go, so
    ///   `ui.open-window` is refused — open a web page with `open.url`
    ///   instead, which is where an uninstall survey belongs.
    ///
    /// `installed` and `updated` run on a thread of their own once the
    /// package has landed, so a Welcome window opened from here comes up
    /// after the install has finished rather than holding it.
    export on-lifecycle: func(event: lifecycle) -> result<_, string>;

    /// Something happened outside the extension that it asked to hear,
    /// by `name`. `payload` is a JSON object with a `"v"` field for its
    /// own version; a new kind of event is a new name, never a new world.
    /// An extension that listens to nothing answers `Ok` and is never
    /// called.
    ///
    /// Heard under the same rules as `on-lifecycle`'s `installed`: only
    /// while switched on, on a thread of its own, the answer written to
    /// Lumi's log and shown to nobody. No window opens from here, and
    /// `clipboard.paste` is refused — both would be the extension acting
    /// on its own, and the person did not press anything. Added in 0.3.
    export on-event: func(name: string, payload: string) -> result<_, string>;
}
